# gap-boundary fixture: `swiftc -c` fix

Commit: `d14231a6` in `boring-wt-architecture` (HEAD moved; this commit adds only
`tests/swift-gap-boundary/gap-boundary.test.ts`).

## 1. Why rc=1

`build.stderr.log` from the failing run (located at
`out/swift-gap-boundary/test-ab05489b-15f7-4a58-bd22-b7ca43accde8/`, the `out`
dir the test derives from `Bun.env.BORING_GAP_BOUNDARY_OUT` or a UUID dir under
`<root>/out/swift-gap-boundary/`) contains exactly one line:

```
error: cannot specify -o when generating multiple output files
```

`swiftc -c` with multiple input files emits one `.o` per input and rejects a
single `-o`. The recorded "one warning at Gap.swift:117" claim was never
reached: the command died at argument parsing. Verified by hand (see
`evidence/hand-run-transcript.txt`): the old invocation reproducibly gives
rc=1 and zero warnings.

## 2. Corrected invocation

```
swiftc -c -whole-module-optimization Gap.swift Runtime.swift UStringException.swift UStringFault.swift -o gap.o
```

WMO produces a single object file, so `-o` is valid, and it still runs SILGen,
so the build-phase warning stays visible.

## 3. New assertions (fixture can now fail)

- `build.exitCode === 0` (the -c step must succeed).
- produced `gap.o` size > 0.
- zero `: error:` lines in build stderr (kept).
- **Pin on the recorded deviation**: exactly one stderr line matching
  `Gap.swift:117` + `will never be executed`. Reproduced under `-c`; recorded,
  not waived — it COUNTS against `02-translator-implementation-standard.md:78/:80`
  per the gate-owner ruling. When the warning is fixed, this assertion fails
  and forces the count to be re-read (same rot-proof pattern as the discharged
  W1 pin). Zero-diagnostics-under-`-c` remains the stated goal.

Note: the raw line-count in `pending-build-warnings.log` is 2 because Swift's
caret-snippet rendering repeats the text `warning:` on the annotation line;
both lines are ONE diagnostic at Gap.swift:117:9. The pin counts
diagnostic-location lines, not raw `warning:` substrings.

## 4. Discriminating proof

- In-fixture: the failure-mode probe now also runs `swiftc -c broken.swift -o`
  on `let broken: Int = "s"` and asserts rc != 0 with `error` in stderr
